// K516 英語版：戦の後の「史実と後日談」（after_history.js の織田家編の三十四戦）。英語の時だけ i18n_menus.js から読む。クラウド29 11/21
//   人と城の名は英字の読み（長音は ō・ū）。伝承は断定しない書き方を元のまま保つ。
import { INOU_AFTER, HISTORY_EN } from './after_history.js';
const INOU_EN = ['Nobunaga\'s side won and Hayashi Mimasaka-no-kami was killed. Nobukatsu and Shibata Katsuie shut themselves in Suemori Castle, and Hayashi Hidesada in Nagoya Castle.',
  'Nobunaga burned the towns below Suemori and Nagoya.',
  'Dota Gozen, mother of Nobunaga and Nobukatsu, begged for their lives.',
  'Nobunaga granted his mother\'s wish and forgave Nobukatsu and the others.',
  'The next year, Katsuie told Nobunaga that Nobukatsu was plotting again. Nobukatsu was killed at Kiyosu Castle, and Katsuie later became a senior Oda vassal and commander of the Hokuriku campaign.'];
export const AFTER_EN = new Map([
  [INOU_AFTER.map((s) => s.text).join('') + '信長は二年後の浮野の戦いで岩倉の織田家も破り、尾張をほぼ一つにまとめた。', INOU_EN.join(' ') + ' Two years later, Nobunaga also beat the Iwakura Oda at the Battle of Ukino and nearly united Owari.'],
  ['織田勢が今川勢を破り、今川義元は討ち取られた。その後、松平元康（のちの徳川家康）は今川家から離れ、信長と手を結んだ。', 'The Oda beat the Imagawa, and Imagawa Yoshimoto was killed. Afterward, Matsudaira Motoyasu (later Tokugawa Ieyasu) left the Imagawa and allied with Nobunaga.'],
  ['織田勢が斎藤勢を破り、敵将の長井甲斐守と日比野下野守が討たれた。ただし美濃をすぐに取れたわけではなく、信長と斎藤家の戦いは続いた。', 'The Oda beat the Saitō, and enemy generals Nagai Kai-no-kami and Hibino Shimotsuke-no-kami were killed. But Mino was not taken at once, and the war with the Saitō went on.'],
  ['藤吉郎が一夜で城を築き、守り切ったという話は後の伝えで、確かな記録では結末を確かめられない。信長はその後も美濃攻めを続け、翌年の1567年に稲葉山城を手に入れた。', 'The tale that Tōkichirō built a castle in one night and held it comes from later accounts; reliable records cannot confirm how it ended. Nobunaga kept up his Mino campaign and took Inabayama Castle the next year, 1567.'],
  ['稲葉山城は織田方のものとなり、斎藤龍興は城を離れた。信長は城下を岐阜と改め、翌年には足利義昭を連れて京へ上った。', 'Inabayama Castle fell to the Oda, and Saitō Tatsuoki left it. Nobunaga renamed the town Gifu, and the next year marched to Kyoto with Ashikaga Yoshiaki.'],
  ['箕作城は織田勢に落とされ、六角父子は観音寺城も捨てて退いた。信長は京へ入り、足利義昭が将軍になった。', 'Mitsukuri Castle fell to the Oda, and the Rokkaku father and son abandoned Kannonji Castle too. Nobunaga entered Kyoto, and Ashikaga Yoshiaki became shōgun.'],
  ['夜の攻めは失敗し、織田勢は囲みを続けて北畠家と和睦した。信長の子の茶筅丸（のちの信雄）が北畠家の養子となり、織田家が伊勢への力を強めた。', 'The night attack failed. The Oda kept up the siege and made peace with the Kitabatake. Nobunaga\'s son Chasenmaru (later Nobukatsu) was adopted into the Kitabatake, and the Oda grew stronger in Ise.'],
  ['浅井長政が朝倉方についたため、信長は攻めをやめて退き、京へ帰った。兵を立て直した織田・徳川勢は、六月に姉川で浅井・朝倉勢と戦った。', 'Because Azai Nagamasa sided with the Asakura, Nobunaga called off the attack and fell back to Kyoto. Regrouped, the Oda and Tokugawa fought the Azai and Asakura at the Ane River in the sixth month.'],
  ['織田・徳川勢が浅井・朝倉勢を破り、横山城も織田方に降った。ただし浅井・朝倉の両家は残り、その年の秋には再び信長を追い詰めた。', 'The Oda and Tokugawa beat the Azai and Asakura, and Yokoyama Castle surrendered to the Oda. But both clans survived, and that autumn they pressed Nobunaga hard again.'],
  ['本願寺も織田方と戦い始め、信長は野田・福島の砦を落とせずに退いた。浅井・朝倉勢が京へ迫ったため近江へ兵を返し、志賀の陣へと続いた。', 'The Hongan-ji also began fighting the Oda, and Nobunaga fell back without taking the Noda and Fukushima forts. With the Azai and Asakura nearing Kyoto, he turned his army to Ōmi, leading to the Shiga campaign.'],
  ['森可成らは討ち死にしたが、残った兵が宇佐山城を守り、城は落ちなかった。信長が戻ると浅井・朝倉勢は比叡山へ退き、十二月の和睦まで向かい合った。', 'Mori Yoshinari and others were killed, but the men left behind held Usayama Castle and it did not fall. When Nobunaga returned, the Azai and Asakura withdrew to Mount Hiei, and both sides faced off until peace in the twelfth month.'],
  ['織田勢は比叡山を焼き討ちし、僧や逃げていた人々も多く命を落とした。その後、明智光秀が坂本城を築き、この地を治める拠点にした。', 'The Oda burned Mount Hiei, and many monks and people who had fled there died. Afterward, Akechi Mitsuhide built Sakamoto Castle as his base to rule the area.'],
  ['徳川・織田勢は武田勢に大敗し、家康は浜松城へ逃げ帰った。信玄はさらに西へ進んだが、翌年に病で亡くなり、武田勢は引き返した。', 'The Tokugawa and Oda were badly beaten by the Takeda, and Ieyasu fled back to Hamamatsu Castle. Shingen pushed further west, but died of illness the next year, and the Takeda turned back.'],
  ['織田勢は刀根坂から越前へ追った。一乗谷は焼かれ、朝倉景鏡は織田方についた。八月二十日、義景は大野で自害し、朝倉家は滅んだ。織田勢は近江へ戻り、次は浅井長政の小谷城を攻めた。', 'The Oda chased the enemy from Tonezaka into Echizen. Ichijōdani was burned, and Asakura Kageakira went over to the Oda. On the 20th day of the eighth month, Yoshikage took his own life at Ōno, and the Asakura clan ended. The Oda returned to Ōmi and next attacked Azai Nagamasa\'s Odani Castle.'],
  ['小谷城は落ち、浅井久政と長政は自害して浅井家は滅んだ。北近江の多くを与えられた秀吉は、のちに長浜城を築いた。', 'Odani Castle fell. Azai Hisamasa and Nagamasa took their own lives, and the Azai clan ended. Hideyoshi, given most of northern Ōmi, later built Nagahama Castle.'],
  ['織田勢が長島の一揆を滅ぼし、焼き討ちなどで戦わない人々も多く命を落とした。翌年、信長は越前の一向一揆にも大軍を向けた。', 'The Oda destroyed the Nagashima uprising, and many non-fighters also died in the burning. The next year, Nobunaga sent a great army against the Ikkō uprising in Echizen too.'],
  ['織田・徳川勢が武田勢を破り、武田方の多くの重臣が討ち死にした。武田家はなお残ったが、徳川勢は反撃を進め、信長は越前攻めへ兵を向けた。', 'The Oda and Tokugawa beat the Takeda, and many senior Takeda vassals were killed. The Takeda clan survived, but the Tokugawa pressed their counterattack and Nobunaga turned his army toward Echizen.'],
  ['織田勢は木ノ芽峠の砦を落とし、越前の一向一揆を崩した。その後、越前の多くは柴田勝家に任され、北陸攻めの拠点となった。', 'The Oda took the forts at Kinome Pass and broke the Ikkō uprising in Echizen. Most of Echizen was then given to Shibata Katsuie and became the base for the Hokuriku campaign.'],
  ['越前攻めで大滝寺も滝川勢に焼かれたと伝わるが、寺での戦いの細かな経過は伝わらず、この戦は言い伝えを核にした補いである。一揆が崩れた後、府中は不破光治・前田利家・佐々成政に任された。', 'It is said Ōtaki-ji was also burned by Takigawa\'s men in the Echizen campaign, but the details of the fighting there are not recorded; this battle is filled in around the legend. After the uprising collapsed, Fuchū was given to Fuwa Mitsuharu, Maeda Toshiie and Sassa Narimasa.'],
  ['十一月十日の夜討ちは退けられ、信忠が城へ追い入った。その後に岩村城は開城し、二十一日に秋山・大島・座光寺が捕らえられ、岐阜で処刑されたと『信長公記』は記す。おつやの方も処刑されたと伝わる。開城と処刑は、この夜の任務とは別の出来事である。翌年五月、次の戦は大坂の天王寺へ移る。', 'The night raid on the 10th day of the eleventh month was driven off, and Nobutada chased the enemy back into the castle. The "Shinchō Kōki" says Iwamura Castle then opened its gates, and on the 21st Akiyama, Ōshima and Zakōji were captured and executed at Gifu. Lady Otsuya is also said to have been executed. The surrender and executions are separate from tonight\'s mission. In the fifth month of the next year, the next battle moves to Tennōji in Osaka.'],
  ['信長は天王寺砦の味方を救い、本願寺勢を押し返した。ただし本願寺は降らず、戦いは1580年の和睦まで続いた。', 'Nobunaga rescued his men in Tennōji Fort and drove back the Hongan-ji forces. But the Hongan-ji did not surrender, and the war went on until peace in 1580.'],
  ['小雑賀川を渡った織田勢は鉄砲で阻まれ、引き返した。その後、信長は雑賀の主な者たちに従うとの誓いを出させ、三月に兵を引いた。', 'The Oda crossed the Kosaika River but were stopped by guns and turned back. Nobunaga then had Saika\'s leaders swear obedience and withdrew in the third month.'],
  ['七尾城を落とした上杉勢が織田勢を破ったと伝わるが、戦の規模や経過には説の違いがある。翌年に上杉謙信が亡くなると、後継ぎ争いが起こり、織田方は北陸で攻めを進めた。', 'It is said the Uesugi, having taken Nanao Castle, beat the Oda, but accounts differ on the battle\'s size and course. When Uesugi Kenshin died the next year, a succession fight broke out, and the Oda advanced in Hokuriku.'],
  ['信貴山城は落ち、松永久秀は自害した。松永家の勢力は崩れ、その後の大和では筒井順慶が力を強めた。', 'Shigisan Castle fell, and Matsunaga Hisahide took his own life. The Matsunaga collapsed, and Tsutsui Junkei grew strong in Yamato.'],
  ['九鬼嘉隆の船団が毛利方の船を退けたが、海からの補給を完全に断ったかには異論もある。本願寺との戦いはなお続き、1580年に和睦した。', 'Kuki Yoshitaka\'s fleet drove off the Mōri ships, though some doubt it fully cut supply by sea. The war with the Hongan-ji went on until peace in 1580.'],
  ['秀吉は大村で別所・毛利勢を破り、三木城への食料の運び込みを阻んだ。飢えが深まった城は翌年に開かれ、別所長治らは城兵の命を助ける代わりに自害した。', 'Hideyoshi beat the Bessho and Mōri at Ōmura and stopped food from reaching Miki Castle. Starving, the castle opened the next year, and Bessho Nagaharu and others took their own lives to spare the garrison.'],
  ['織田勢が有岡城の外側へ攻め入り、本丸も十一月に開け渡された。黒田官兵衛は救い出されたが、荒木村重の妻子や家臣の家族の多くは処刑された。', 'The Oda broke into the outer works of Arioka Castle, and the main bailey was handed over in the eleventh month. Kuroda Kanbei was rescued, but Araki Murashige\'s wife and children and many of his retainers\' families were executed.'],
  ['比自山の守り手は城を捨てて退いたと伝わり、織田勢は伊賀各地を攻め進んだ。柏原城も和睦して開かれ、伊賀は織田方に従うことになった。', 'The defenders of Hijiyama are said to have abandoned the castle, and the Oda advanced across Iga. Kashiwabara Castle also opened after a truce, and Iga came under the Oda.'],
  ['食料を断たれた鳥取城は、吉川経家が城内の人々の命を助ける代わりに自害して開かれた。その後、秀吉方の宮部継潤が鳥取を治めた。次の戦は翌年三月、信忠が攻める信濃の高遠城へ移る。', 'Cut off from food, Tottori Castle opened when Kikkawa Tsuneie took his own life to spare the people inside. Hideyoshi\'s man Miyabe Keijun then ruled Tottori. The next battle moves to the third month of the next year, at Takatō Castle in Shinano, attacked by Nobutada.'],
  ['高遠城はその日のうちに落ち、仁科盛信は討ち死にした。織田勢は甲斐へ進み、三月十一日に武田勝頼らが田野で自害して武田家は滅んだ。', 'Takatō Castle fell that same day, and Nishina Morinobu was killed. The Oda advanced into Kai, and on the 11th day of the third month Takeda Katsuyori and others took their own lives at Tano, ending the Takeda clan.'],
  ['武田勝頼と信勝は田野で自害し、武田家は滅んだ。信長は武田の旧領を家臣や家康に分けたが、六月の本能寺の変でその支配は揺らいだ。', 'Takeda Katsuyori and Nobukatsu took their own lives at Tano, and the Takeda clan ended. Nobunaga split the old Takeda lands among his vassals and Ieyasu, but his hold shook after the Honnō-ji Incident in the sixth month.'],
  ['明智光秀の攻撃で信長は本能寺で命を絶ち、信忠も二条御所で自害した。中国地方から引き返した秀吉が、六月十三日の山崎の戦いで光秀を破った。', 'Attacked by Akechi Mitsuhide, Nobunaga died at Honnō-ji, and Nobutada took his own life at Nijō Palace. Hideyoshi, hurrying back from the Chūgoku region, beat Mitsuhide at the Battle of Yamazaki on the 13th day of the sixth month.'],
]);
// 豊臣・徳川・武田・上杉編の戦の history（各戦の def.history）。字が後で直されても効くよう、戦の id で引く（after_history.js の afterHistory が英語の時に使う）
const BY_ID = [
  ["t01_yamazaki", "Eleven days after the Honnō-ji Incident, about 40,000 Hashiba troops, racing back from Bitchū-Takamatsu, met about 16,000 Akechi troops on the narrow plain between Mount Tennō and the Yodo River. On a rainy evening, Ise Sadaoki attacked Nakagawa Kiyohide's unit and the battle began, and Saitō Toshimitsu crossed the Enmyōji River to push Takayama Ukon's unit. Hori Hidemasa arrived in support and held, while on Mount Tennō, Hashiba Hidenaga and Kuroda Kanbei fought Matsuda Masachika and Namikawa Yasuie. Then Ikeda Tsuneoki and others crossed the shallows along the Yodo, broke Tsuda Nobuharu's unit and struck the Akechi flank. The Akechi army collapsed. While Mimaki Kaneaki held the rear and died, Mitsuhide fell back from his camp at Gobōzuka to Shōryūji Castle. Leaving the castle at night for Sakamoto, Mitsuhide was killed at Ogurusu."],
  ["t02_shizugatake", "In Tenshō 11, Hashiba Hideyoshi and Shibata Katsuie faced each other from forts around Lake Yogo in Ōmi. Shibata's side, including Sakuma Morimasa and Maeda Toshiie, held Mount Gyōichi, Bessho and Shigeyama in the north; Hashiba's side held Dōgi, Shinmei, Higashino, Iwasaki, Ōiwa, Shizugatake and Tagami in the south."],
  ["t03_komaki", "In Tenshō 12 (1584), Oda Nobukatsu allied with Tokugawa Ieyasu and broke with Hashiba Hideyoshi. When Ikeda Tsuneoki, on Hideyoshi's side, took Inuyama Castle, Ieyasu seized Komakiyama and had it fortified with moats and earthworks. At dawn on the 17th day of the third month, Mori Nagayoshi, camped at Haguro to strike Komakiyama, was hit from the front by the vanguard of Okudaira Nobumasa and Sakakibara Yasumasa and from the side by Sakai Tadatsugu, who had circled around. He lost about 300 men and fell back to Inuyama (the Battle of Haguro; about 5,000 on the Tokugawa side). In the retreat, the horo guardsman Noro Sukezaemon and his son turned back and were killed. At the end of the third month, Hideyoshi set his camp at Gakuden and lined up forts such as Nijūbori, facing Ieyasu and Nobukatsu at Komakiyama. Neither side left its forts, and small fights, like night raids on the front-line Nijūbori fort (held by Hineno Hironari), went on. To break the deadlock, Ikeda Tsuneoki proposed a raid deep into Mikawa while Ieyasu was away. The distance between Haguro and Nijūbori is shortened in this reconstruction, and your squad's moves are a story made for the game."],
  ["t03b_nagakute", "During the standoff with Ieyasu at Komaki, on Ikeda Tsuneoki's plan, about 20,000 men under Ikeda Tsuneoki and Motosuke, Mori Nagayoshi, Hori Hidemasa and Miyoshi Nobuyoshi (Hideyoshi's nephew, renamed Hashiba Hidetsugu the next year) swung east to strike Mikawa while Ieyasu was away. Learning of it, Ieyasu left Komakiyama in pursuit, and on the morning of the 9th day of the fourth month he attacked Nobuyoshi's resting unit at Hakusanbayashi from behind and the side and broke it. Nobuyoshi escaped after losing his horse. Hori Hidemasa lined up guns at Hinokigane and drove back the pursuers, but withdrew on learning that Ieyasu's main army had got behind Ikeda and Mori. Ieyasu and Nobukatsu set their camp on Mihatayama (Fujigane), between Hori and the Ikeda-Mori force, raised the golden fan standard, and sent Ii Naomasa's red unit and others forward. At Nagakute the Ikeda and Mori troops were enveloped from front and side. Mori Nagayoshi was shot between the eyes, Ikeda Motosuke was killed by Andō Naotsugu, and Tsuneoki by Nagai Naokatsu. Unable to beat Ieyasu in battle, Hideyoshi made peace with Nobukatsu in the eleventh month that year and came to terms with Ieyasu too. The distances and directions between Hakusanbayashi, Hinokigane and Hotokegane are shortened in this reconstruction, and your squad's moves are a story made for the game. No record says fences or moats were built at Hinokigane, so none are placed. The course of the Kanare River and the paddies west of Hotokegane are estimated from the terrain."],
  ["t04_negoro", "In the third month of Tenshō 13 (1585), having made peace after Komaki and Nagakute, Hashiba Hideyoshi marched on Kishū with about 100,000 men to attack the Negoro and Saika bands, famed for their guns. On the 21st, the main Negoro force fought fiercely at Sengokubori Castle in Izumi, but fire reached the powder store and the castle fell. On the 23rd, Hideyoshi's army entered Negoro-ji, and most of its lodges burned. The Great Pagoda and the Daishi Hall survived, and the pagoda is said to still bear bullet marks. Historically, there was little resistance at Negoro-ji itself. In the game, the gunners left at the temple resist more strongly (firing from lodge verandas, lookouts, the great gate's tower and the walls), and the advance behind bamboo bundles, defeating the gunners aiming at Hori Hidemasa, breaking the great gate with a log, storming the grounds and defending the pagoda are joined into one series of stages. The terrain shrinks the Geospatial Information Authority's survey (Kitayama, Maeyama and the great gate's entrance), and the halls are placed to match today's Great Gate, Daidenbō Hall, Great Pagoda and Daishi Hall. The sub-temples sit on terraces cut into the slope with stone walls (from excavations). Sizes, the number of sub-temples, and the posts of Hidetsugu and Tsutsui Sadatsugu are estimates."],
  ["t05_ichinomiya", "In the sixth month of Tenshō 13 (1585), Hashiba Hideyoshi sent a great army to Shikoku under his brother Hidenaga, together with his nephew Hidetsugu and others. Crossing to Awa, Hidenaga surrounded Ichinomiya Castle, held by Chōsokabe Motochika's retainers Tani Tadazumi and Emura Chikatoshi.\nHidenaga is said to have cut off the castle's water, and the garrison suffered from thirst. In the seventh month, Tani Tadazumi opened the castle and went back to Motochika to urge surrender. Motochika gave in and was allowed to keep only Tosa.\nThe placement of forts, water sources and ridge paths, the size of both armies, and the dialogue are estimates made for the game."],
  ["t07_ganjaku", "The 1st day of the fourth month, Tenshō 15 (1587). Entering Kyushu, Toyotomi Hideyoshi's main army attacked Ganjaku Castle in Buzen, held by Akizuki Tanezane. Hideyoshi camped at Umagatake and did not come to Mount Ganjaku. His adopted son Hashiba Hidekatsu led the attack, with Gamō Ujisato at the front gate and Maeda Toshikatsu (later Toshinaga) at the back gate, about 5,000 attackers in all.\nThe castle stood on the summit of Mount Ganjaku (454 m), a steep mountain castle whose great boulders were natural defenses. The Akizuki garrison of about 3,000 is said to have held the front gate under Akuta Akurokubei and the back gate under the castle commander Kumai Etchū-no-kami. The attack began before dawn, with guns firing without pause, and the castle fell before sunset. About 400 of the garrison were killed, Kumai Etchū-no-kami among them.\nWith a strong castle falling so fast, Akizuki Tanezane abandoned Masutomi Castle for Mount Koshosan and soon surrendered without a fight. The Toyotomi advance through Kyushu sped up at once."],
  ["t08_nejirozaka", "In Tenshō 15 (1587), Toyotomi Hidenaga's great army surrounded Taka Castle in Hyūga. Miyabe Keijun and about 10,000 men built a fort with dry moats and board walls at Nejirozaka, south of Taka Castle, to wait for the Shimazu relief. On the night of the 17th day of the fourth month, about 35,000 under Shimazu Yoshihisa and Yoshitomo (later Yoshihiro) threw everything at the fort, but Miyabe's men held with the moats, walls and guns. Hidenaga's main army held back from relief on Bitō Tomonobu's advice, but Tōdō Takatora's and Togawa Michiyasu's men came to help, and Kuroda Kanbei (Yoshitaka) and Kobayakawa Takakage struck the Shimazu flank. Shimazu Tadachika, Saruwatari Nobumitsu and others were killed, and the Shimazu withdrew. In the fifth month, Yoshihisa shaved his head and surrendered to Hideyoshi. Accounts of each army's size differ."],
  ["t09_yamanaka", "In Tenshō 18 (1590), Toyotomi Hideyoshi attacked the Hōjō of Odawara. On the morning of the 29th day of the third month, about 70,000 men under Toyotomi Hidetsugu attacked Yamanaka Castle on the western slope of Hakone. About 4,000 under Matsuda Yasunaga held the castle, strengthened with moats divided by ridges on the bottom (shōji-bori and une-bori) and bent gate entrances. Hitotsuyanagi Naosue of the attackers was shot and killed, but the men of Nakamura Kazuuji (whose retainer Watanabe Kanbei was first in), Hori Hidemasa, Yamauchi Kazutoyo and others climbed in from several sides, and the castle fell in half a day. Mamiya Yasutoshi, who held the Daisaki outwork, and the castle lord Matsuda Yasunaga were both killed. The Hōjō western defense collapsed in a day, and Hideyoshi's army crossed Hakone and surrounded Odawara. Accounts of troop numbers differ."],
  ["t10_oshi", "In Tenshō 18 (1590), during Toyotomi Hideyoshi's Odawara campaign, Ishida Mitsunari surrounded the Hōjō's Oshi Castle in Musashi. The castle was a \"floating castle\" ringed by marshes and deep paddies. Mitsunari built a long levee (the Ishida Levee) and diverted river water to flood it. The castle did not sink, and it is said heavy rain broke the levee and flooded the attackers' side instead (a diary of the time records no heavy rain, so whether rain or people broke it is uncertain). The defenders held out and surrendered the castle only after Odawara opened. The attackers are said to have numbered about 23,000 and the defenders 3,116 (69 samurai, 420 ashigaru, and the rest farmers and townspeople). On the rainy night of the 18th day of the sixth month, it is said the men of Honjō Yasunobu at the Shimo-Oshi gate, fearing the water would reach the main bailey, cut the levee, killing about 270 attackers. Your section, the number and size of night raids, defenders approaching the levee by boat, and the details of repairing the breach are reconstructed for the game. The breach is treated as a mix of heavy rain, water pressure and defenders' interference, not one person's deed. The directions and distance between Oshi Castle and Marubakayama are shrunk from the real positions. The defenders' banner is the Narita clan's crest of three bars under the moon. Ōtani Yoshitsugu and Natsuka Masaie joined the attackers, and in the seventh month Asano Nagayoshi (later Nagamasa), Sanada Masayuki and others also attacked the gates, but the castle did not fall. The lord, Narita Ujinaga, was shut up in Odawara; after the castle warden Narita Yasusue died of illness during the siege, his kinsman Narita Nagachika held the castle, with Masaki Tanba-no-kami and others guarding the gates. The tale that Ujinaga's daughter Princess Kai rode out to fight is a later legend from war chronicles (kept apart from history and not used in this battle). Letters of the time show the flooding was Hideyoshi's order, and that Mitsunari actually wanted to attack first. In early seventh month Asano Nagayoshi broke the Sarao gate and took over thirty heads, but Hideyoshi replied, \"Flood it, whatever happens.\" After Odawara opened, Oshi Castle was handed over on the 16th day of the seventh month on Ujinaga's word. It is said the defenders and local people were not punished. The terrain is based on the Geospatial Information Authority's elevation data."],
  ["t11_hachioji", "In the Odawara campaign of Tenshō 18 (1590), the Toyotomi side attacked the Hōjō castles of the Kantō one after another. Hachiōji Castle was a mountain castle built by Hōjō Ujiteru, with baileys running from the main bailey on the summit of Mount Fukasawa down to the lord's residence (Goshuden) in the valley of the Shiroyama River. Ujiteru was shut up in Odawara, so the castle was held by the warden Yokochi Kenmotsu, Nakayama Kageyu (Ienori), Kanō Ichian and others, and besides soldiers many women and children from nearby villages had taken refuge there. Before dawn on the 23rd day of the sixth month, about 15,000 under Maeda Toshiie and Toshinaga, Uesugi Kagekatsu, Sanada Masayuki and others, with Daidōji Masashige and others who had already surrendered as the vanguard, attacked through fog from the front and back gates. Guided by a turncoat, the Uesugi at the back gate took a hidden path to strike the summit baileys, and the castle fell in a day. Nakayama Kageyu refused Toshiie's offer to spare him and was killed; the warden Yokochi Kenmotsu escaped. It is said women threw themselves into the waterfall at the Goshuden. The fall of Hachiōji was reported to Odawara and helped break the Hōjō will.\nThe layout and size of the baileys are shrunk for the game, and the ambush from the cedar woods in the valley, the defenders' sally from the Goshuden, and the Uesugi route are estimates for the game. Other accounts give different troop numbers."],
  ["t12_odawara", "In Tenshō 18 (1590), Toyotomi Hideyoshi attacked the Hōjō of the Kantō with about 210,000 men. The year before, the Hōjō seizure of the Sanada's Nagurumi Castle was held to break Hideyoshi's peace ruling, and this started the war. Daimyo from across the land, including Tokugawa Ieyasu, Maeda Toshiie, Uesugi Kagekatsu, Ukita Hideie and Kobayakawa Takakage, took part, and fleets under Kuki Yoshitaka, Chōsokabe Motochika and others blocked the sea. Hōjō Ujimasa and Ujinao shut themselves in with over 50,000 men inside Odawara's great outer wall (about 9 km), which enclosed the whole town with moats and earthworks. Hideyoshi did not launch a general assault, and built a castle on Mount Ishigaki overlooking it. Ieyasu camped east of the castle, between the Sannō and Sakawa rivers. On the rainy night of the 22nd day of the sixth month, Ieyasu's retainers Ii Naomasa and Matsudaira Yasuyori crossed the moat, attacked the Shino-guruwa outwork beyond the Sannō gate and raised their banner, but were held off by Hōjō troops from the castle and, without backup, withdrew. It was almost the only time anyone broke into the castle during the Odawara siege. Around the 26th, they are said to have cut the trees around the Ishigaki castle so it seemed to appear overnight (in fact it took about eighty days to build). Meanwhile the branch castles of Yamanaka, Nirayama, Matsuida, Hachigata, Hachiōji and others fell one after another, and the betrayal by senior retainer Matsuda Norihide came to light, shaking the castle. Kuroda Kanbei (Yoshitaka), Takigawa Katsutoshi and others urged surrender (Kanbei is said to have entered the castle in formal dress without a sword), and on the 5th day of the seventh month Ujinao surrendered, offering his own life to save those in the castle. On the 11th Ujimasa and his brother Ujiteru took their own lives, and Ujinao was sent to Mount Kōya. On the 13th Hideyoshi entered the castle; the old Hōjō lands of the Kantō were given to Tokugawa Ieyasu, who moved to Edo.\nThe shape of Shino-guruwa, the numbers of Ii's men and of the outwork's defenders, your joining Ii's unit, and the route escorting Kanbei to the main bailey gate are reconstructions for the game. Troop numbers are estimates, and other accounts give different Hōjō numbers."],
  ["k01_fushimi", "In Keichō 5 (1600), before setting out east to attack Uesugi Kagekatsu of Aizu, Tokugawa Ieyasu left Fushimi Castle, at the gateway to Kyoto, to his retainer Torii Mototada. Tradition says Ieyasu asked him to hold the castle to the death, and the two shared a farewell cup. In the seventh month, Ishida Mitsunari and others raised troops and the Western Army formed. On the 19th the garrison sallied out and burned the mansions of Natsuka Masaie and Maeda Gen'i outside the castle. The attackers grew day by day: Ukita Hideie joined on the 22nd and Kobayakawa Hideaki and others on the 23rd, and with Mōri Hidemoto, Kikkawa Hiroie, Konishi Yukinaga, Nabeshima Katsushige, Natsuka Masaie and others they reached about 40,000 (the attack is said to have begun on the 19th or the 25th). They shelled the castle day and night with cannon on built-up mounds and guns, and filled the moats to close in. The castle held only about 1,800, including Torii Mototada (main bailey), Matsudaira Ietada and Matsudaira Chikamasa (third bailey), Naitō Ienaga and his son Motonaga (west bailey), Fukao Seijūrō and the Kōka men (Matsu bailey), Kanbayashi Chikuan of a Uji tea-master family (Taiko bailey) and Sano Tsunamasa, yet they held out more than ten days. At midday on the 1st day of the eighth month, a fire started in the Matsu bailey through the Kōka men's treachery, and the attackers stormed in from all sides. Mototada, lame, is said to have fought leaning on his sword, and it is also said Saika Magoichi (Suzuki Shigetomo) respectfully waited for him to take his own life before taking his head. The castle burned and fell (said to be around noon or in the evening). The defenders' dead are given as three hundred-odd, eight hundred, or all of them. The floorboards stained with their blood were later raised into the ceilings of temples in Kyoto, where they remain as \"blood ceilings\". This delay bought time for Ieyasu to return from the east and advance into Mino.\nThe tale that Shimazu Yoshihiro first tried to enter the castle and was refused comes from Edo-period books and is uncertain. The layout and size of the baileys are shrunk for the game, and details of the betrayal and Mototada's death vary by account."],
  ["k02_kawada", "In the eighth month of Keichō 5 (1600), the Eastern Army gathered at Kiyosu decided to cross the Kiso River into Mino to attack Oda Hidenobu at Gifu Castle. On the 22nd, Ikeda Terumasa (later Terumasa), Asano Yoshinaga, Yamauchi Kazutoyo and others crossed at the Kawada ferry, while Fukushima Masanori, Kuroda Nagamasa, Hosokawa Tadaoki and others crossed downstream at Okoshi. On the north bank at Kawada, the Oda men Kozukuri Nagamasa and Dodo Tsunaie lined up guns, with further defenses downstream at Komeno village and north at Shinkanō (Hidenobu was at his camp at the Enma Hall in Kawate). The Eastern Army numbered about 18,000, the Oda about 3,000. The Eastern Army crossed at dawn and camped on the sandbar of Koyabajima, crossed the northern channel too, and fought at Komeno at midday. Ōtsuka Gondayū, a retainer of Hitotsuyanagi Naomori, crossed first but was killed by the young warrior Iinuma Nagasuke, who also died there. Kozukuri and Dodo fell back to Gifu Castle. The Oda also tried to hold at Shinkanō but were beaten, and Hidenobu too withdrew to Gifu Castle. This led to the attack on Gifu Castle the next day.\nThe Kiso River's course was very different then; around Kawashima it split into many channels around sandbars."],
  ["k03_gifu", "In the eighth month of Keichō 5 (1600), the Eastern Army gathered at Kiyosu in Owari crossed the Kiso River into Mino. On the 22nd, Ikeda Terumasa and others crossed from Kawada and beat Oda Hidenobu's troops at Komeno, while Fukushima Masanori and others gave up on the Okoshi ferry, crossed downstream at Kaganoi and took Takegahana Castle. The castle lord, Oda Hidenobu, was Oda Nobunaga's grandson (Sanbōshi). He sided with the West, shut himself in Gifu Castle and asked Ōgaki and Inuyama for relief.\nOn the morning of the 23rd, Asano Yoshinaga, Hitotsuyanagi Naomori and others took the fort on Mount Zuiryūji to the southwest, and the Ikeda and Fukushima troops rushed the paths up to the castle. The main path, Nanamagari, climbs from the south, Hyakumagari from the lord's residence at the western foot, and the water path from the northwest. The defenders placed Tsuda Tōzaburō at the main town gate, Kozukuri Nagamasa and his father at Nanamagari, and Dodo Tsunaie at Hyakumagari. Fukushima Masanori attacked Nanamagari, and Terumasa is said to have climbed the water path, though letters of the time do not mention it. Terumasa and Masanori competed to be first in, and it is said Terumasa yielded and called it a tie. That same morning Kuroda Nagamasa and others beat the relief from Ōgaki at the Gōdo River.\nEverything but the main bailey fell in a day. Hidenobu was about to take his own life but was talked out of it by Terumasa, Masanori and others, surrendered through Terumasa, shaved his head at Entoku-ji in Kanō, and was spared and sent to Mount Kōya. The next day the Eastern Army advanced to Akasaka toward Ishida Mitsunari at Ōgaki Castle. The commanders Dodo Tsunaie and Kozukuri Nagamasa also surrendered. The castle's floorboards are said to have later become the blood ceiling at Sōfuku-ji. The layout and size of the baileys are shrunk estimates for the game, and the Ikeda line of attack and the commanders assigned to each path are estimates."],
  ["k04_kuisegawa", "On the 14th day of the ninth month of Keichō 5 (1600), Ishida Mitsunari's Western Army came out of Ōgaki Castle to face Tokugawa Ieyasu's Eastern Army, newly arrived at Akasaka in Mino. About 500 men under Shima Sakon and Gamō Bitchū crossed the Kuise River and cut the rice right in front of the Eastern Army to provoke it, then pretended to flee when chased. When Nakamura Kazuhide's unit crossed the river in pursuit, Akashi Takenori's men (a retainer of Ukita Hideie), hidden in the reeds, and Sakon's returning troops fell on them, and Nakamura's chief retainer Noishiki Tanomo, stuck in a deep paddy, was killed. Arima Toyouji's unit covered them and the Eastern Army withdrew. The Western Army is said to have had 1,300 men and the Eastern 6,000 (accounts differ). This small win raised Western morale, but the decisive battle came the next day, the 15th, at Sekigahara.\nBecause the river has shifted, the exact positions on the field are uncertain."],
  ["k05_ueda", "In Keichō 5 (1600), Tokugawa Hidetada, turning back from the Aizu campaign, led 38,000 men west along the Nakasendō. At Ueda Castle in Shinano, Sanada Masayuki and his second son Nobushige, on the Toyotomi (Western) side, held out with about 2,500. The eldest son Nobuyuki was on the Tokugawa side, so the Sanada were split between father and younger son on one side and elder son on the other.\nHidetada urged surrender, but Masayuki stalled his reply to buy time. On the 5th day of the ninth month, when Nobuyuki marched on Toishi Castle, Nobushige avoided fighting his brother and withdrew to Ueda Castle. Around the 6th, Hidetada had Makino Yasunari and others cut the rice below the castle, which led to skirmishes with Sanada troops who came out. Honda Tadamasa's and Ōkubo Tadachika's men chased them to the main gate, but were driven back by the castle's guns and hidden troops, and Hidetada pulled his men back.\nUeda Castle did not fall. Hidetada received Ieyasu's messenger ordering him to hurry to Mino and rushed west, but missed the decisive battle at Sekigahara on the 15th. Details of the fighting (hidden troops, breaking the Kami River weir) come from war chronicles, and some see it as only a skirmish.\nIn this game, Ieyasu's urgent messenger arrives in the middle of the siege (in fact he reached Hidetada around the 8th)."],
  ["k06_sekigahara", "When Toyotomi Hideyoshi died in Keichō 3 (1598), the rivalry grew between Tokugawa Ieyasu of the Five Elders and Ishida Mitsunari of the Five Commissioners. In Keichō 5, while Ieyasu was away attacking Uesugi Kagekatsu of Aizu, Mitsunari and others raised troops, and on the morning of the 15th day of the ninth month, about 75,000 of the Eastern Army and about 80,000 of the Western Army (only about half of whom actually fought) clashed at Sekigahara in Mino."],
  ["k07_sawayama", "On the 15th day of the ninth month of Keichō 5 (1600), after the Western defeat at Sekigahara, the Eastern Army headed for Mitsunari's base, Sawayama Castle. It was a mountain castle with a five-story keep on the summit of Sawayama overlooking Lake Biwa. Mitsunari did not return; his father Ishida Masatsugu, his brother Masazumi and Masazumi's son Tomonari, and Mitsunari's father-in-law Uda Yoritada held it with about 2,800 men. Ieyasu camped on Mount Shōbō south of the castle, and from dawn on the 17th about 15,000 under Kobayakawa Hideaki as vanguard, with Wakisaka Yasuharu, Kutsuki Mototsuna, Ogawa Suketada, Akaza Naoyasu, Ii Naomasa, Tanaka Yoshimasa and others, attacked from the front and back gates. When the attack stopped at dusk, Hasegawa Moritomo, who held the third bailey, led his men out of the castle and guided the Eastern Army, and the castle fell on the 18th. Masatsugu, Masazumi, Tomonari, Uda Yoritada and Mitsunari's wife are said to have taken their own lives (another account says Masazumi was killed). Mitsunari himself escaped after Sekigahara and was captured in Ōmi by Tanaka Yoshimasa's men on the 21st. Mitsunari's son Shigeie became a monk and survived.\nThe layout and size of the baileys are shrunk for the game, and the detailed attack routes are estimates. Some points, such as tales of betrayal, differ between sources."],
  ["k08_osaka_kizu", "In Keichō 19 (1614), the inscription on the Hōkō-ji bell split the Tokugawa and Toyotomi, and Toyotomi Hideyori gathered masterless samurai and shut himself in Osaka Castle. In the eleventh month, Tokugawa Ieyasu began surrounding Osaka with about 200,000 men. The Toyotomi placed forts outside the castle at the Kizu River mouth, Bakurōbuchi, Noda, Fukushima, Shigino, Imafuku and elsewhere.\nThe Kizu River mouth fort controlled the boat route from Osaka Bay into the castle, and about 800 of Akashi Takenori's men held it. Hachisuka Yoshishige's scouts reported little cooking smoke from the fort, and Ieyasu ordered about 3,000 under Hachisuka, Asano Nagaakira and Ikeda Tadakatsu to attack. They were to set out at dawn, but the Hachisuka force jumped the gun and left around 3 a.m. on the 19th.\nThe boat force came up the Kizu River while a land force went around behind the fort and set fire to houses, trapping it between water and land. The late Asano force hurried across the river and many drowned. Takenori was away at a council in the castle; his kinsman Akashi Zen'en held the fort, but orders fell apart, the fort fell that same day, and the defenders fled toward Bakurōbuchi."],
  ["k09_imafuku", "On the 26th day of the eleventh month of Keichō 19 (1614), there was fighting at Imafuku and Shigino, northeast of Osaka Castle. At Imafuku, a narrow path on top of a levee was the only way through, with marsh and water on both sides. Before dawn, Satake Yoshinobu's unit crept up under cover of the levee, broke through the four rows of fences of the Toyotomi fort and took it; the commanders Yano Masanori and Iida Iesada were killed. Kimura Shigenari's unit counterattacked, and when Gotō Matabei's unit joined by levee and boat on Hideyori's order, the Satake were pushed back, their chief retainer Shibue Masamitsu was shot dead, and their vanguard broke. When Satake Yoshinobu called for help, Uesugi Kagekatsu's army at Shigino and the units of Horio Tadaharu and Sakakibara Yasukatsu fired from a sandbar in the Yamato River; Matabei was wounded by an Uesugi bullet, and the Toyotomi withdrew.\nThe position and width of the levee are estimated. Accounts differ on details of the fighting."],
  ["k10_shigino", "The 26th day of the eleventh month of Keichō 19 (1614), the Osaka Winter Campaign. Shigino, east of Osaka Castle, was low marsh, and large armies could only move along the levee by the Yamato River. The Toyotomi built three rows of fences on the levee and had Inoue Yoritsugu hold them.\nUesugi Kagekatsu's vanguard under Suda Nagayoshi and Yasuda Yoshimoto took the fences one by one, and Inoue Yoritsugu was killed. From the castle, 12,000 under Ōno Harunaga, Watanabe Tadasu, Takeda Eiō, Kimura Muneaki, Anazawa Morihide and others came to help, and the Uesugi vanguard was pushed back to the first fence. The second unit under Suibara Chikanori fired guns all at once from both sides of the levee, and Yasuda Yoshimoto charged in with spears and pushed them back.\nIeyasu ordered the Uesugi to fall back, rest and swap with Horio Tadaharu, but Kagekatsu refused to retreat in the middle of a battle. With help from Horio, Niwa Nagashige, Sakakibara Yasukatsu and others, the Toyotomi withdrew into the castle. That same day, when Satake Yoshinobu and others were pressed by Kimura Shigenari and Gotō Mototsugu at Imafuku north of the Yamato River, the Uesugi gunners fired across the river from a sandbar and helped the Satake."],
  ["k11_sanadamaru", "In the Osaka Winter Campaign of Keichō 19 (1614), Sanada Nobushige built an outwork south of Osaka Castle, outside the outer wall, later called the Sanada-maru. It had a fence outside, a dry moat inside it, and walls and towers on an earthwork, with guns lined up behind the walls. On the 4th day of the twelfth month, the men of Maeda Toshimitsu (later Toshitsune), who had been harassed by Sanada guns from Sasayama in front, advanced on Sasayama, but the Sanada troops had already left. Provoked, the Maeda force rushed up to the outwork's dry moat and was shot to pieces from the walls and towers. The troops of Ii Naotaka and Matsudaira Tadanao, mistaking a powder explosion inside the castle for a traitor's signal, also rushed the moat and were likewise shot down. Tokugawa losses are said to have reached several thousand, though accounts differ. After this Ieyasu gave up direct assault, shelled the castle with cannon and pushed for peace.\nThe outwork's size is shrunk for the game, and its shape (half-circle or square) is debated. The position of Sasayama and the taunting words come from tradition."],
  ["k12_kashii", "In the fourth month of Keichō 20 (1615), Osaka Castle's moats had been filled and war broke out again (the Summer Campaign). Asano Nagaakira of Kishū marched north with about 5,000 men, but on spotting the vanguard of Ōno Harufusa's army he decided to fall back to Kashii, leaving Kameda Takatsuna at Yasumatsu as rear guard. Around 8 a.m. on the 29th, Ban Dan'emon (Naoyuki)'s men came up the Kumano road, and the fighting began at Hatchōnawate in Yasumatsu. Kameda fired and fell back, drawing the enemy into Kashii village. In the village the two sides became tangled, and Ueda Sōko, Asano Tomochika and others joined. Okabe Noritsuna attacked from the side but withdrew, and Ban, Tannowa Shigemasa and twelve named men were killed. Harufusa's main army never moved from Kaizuka and pulled back to Osaka. Later Kameda and Ueda quarreled over who had killed Dan'emon, and Kameda left the Asano house."],
  ["k13_domyoji", "The 6th day of the fifth month of Keichō 20 (1615), the Osaka Summer Campaign. The Toyotomi planned to meet the Tokugawa army coming to Osaka along the Yamato road (about 34,000 including vanguard commanders Mizuno Katsunari, Honda Tadamasa, Date Masamune and Katakura Shigetsuna; supreme commander Matsudaira Tadateru) at the mouth of the narrow pass at Kokubu. Gotō Matabei (about 2,800) did not wait for Susukida Kanesuke, Sanada Nobushige, Mōri Katsunaga and others, crossed the Ishikawa River in the fog and went ahead to Komatsuyama. At the hour of the tiger (about 4 a.m.), Matabei broke the vanguard of Matsukura Shigemasa and Okuda Tadatsugu climbing the hill and killed Okuda, but Mizuno Katsunari, Hori Naoyori and others came to help and, joined by the Date, surrounded the hill. With the following troops delayed by fog, Matabei was cut off and fought more than ten times his number until midday before he was killed. Susukida Kanesuke, Yamakawa Katanobu, Akashi Takenori and others who arrived later were also beaten near Dōmyōji, and Kanesuke was killed. Nobushige and Katsunaga, arriving at midday, traded gunfire with the Date at Konda and pushed them back with an ambush. Around 2:30 p.m. word came of the defeat at Yao and Wakae, and around 4 p.m. Nobushige held the rear while they withdrew to Osaka. The Tokugawa troops were exhausted and did not pursue.\nNumbers and detailed troop movements vary by source, and the terrain is a shrunk estimate for the game."],
  ["k14_yao_wakae", "On the 6th day of the fifth month of Keichō 20 (1615), in the Osaka Summer Campaign, the Osaka side left the castle to meet the Tokugawa among the rice fields of Kawachi. That same day at Dōmyōji, Gotō Matabei was also killed. At Wakae, about 4,700 under Kimura Shigenari clashed head-on with about 3,200 of Ii Naotaka's red unit. Kimura's men drove back the Tōdō right wing, but in the fight with the Ii, Yamaguchi Hirosada, Naitō Nagaaki and others were killed, and Shigenari himself was killed by Andō Shigekatsu (Chōzaburō) of the Ii house. He was twenty-three. At Yao, about 5,300 under Chōsokabe Morichika hid along the levee of the Nagase River and struck the advancing vanguard of Tōdō Takatora all at once, breaking it; Tōdō Takanori, Kuwana Yoshinari and other Tōdō commanders were killed one after another. But when Kimura's force at Wakae collapsed, Chōsokabe, fearing an attack on his flank, withdrew to Osaka. Both the Tōdō and Ii suffered heavy losses and were taken out of the vanguard for the next day's battle at Tennōji and Okayama.\nThe tale that Ieyasu praised the incense burned into Shigenari's helmet at the head inspection is an anecdote from later books. Your squad's moves and the distance between the two fields (really a little over 2 km) are a story for the game, and troop numbers vary by source."],
  ["k15_tennoji_okayama", "The 7th day of the fifth month of Keichō 20 (1615), the last field battle of the Osaka Summer Campaign. The Tokugawa had about 150,000, with Ieyasu taking the Tennōji approach and Hidetada the Okayama approach. About 55,000 Toyotomi troops sallied out south of the castle. At Tennōji, Mōri Katsunaga, camped before the south gate of Shitennō-ji (about 6,500 including the remnants of Kimura and Gotō), broke the vanguard of Honda Tadatomo and Ogasawara Hidemasa, and Tadatomo was killed. Sanada Nobushige, camped on Chausuyama (about 3,500), let Matsudaira Tadanao's Echizen army (about 15,000) in front of him pass his camp toward the castle, then charged straight at Ieyasu's headquarters (about 15,000 bannermen). The headquarters broke, the standard fell, and Ieyasu is said to have briefly fled. Soon Matsudaira Tadanao's returning Echizen troops and others pressed from the side; the exhausted Sanada unit collapsed, and Nobushige was killed by Nishio Munetsugu of Echizen near the Yasui Tenjin shrine (or on the rise between Ikutama and Shōman). Katsunaga withdrew into the castle, and at Okayama too Ōno Harufusa and others were broken by Maeda, Ii and Tōdō troops. Osaka Castle went up in flames, and the next day, the 8th, Hideyori and Lady Yodo took their own lives in the Yamazato bailey, ending the Toyotomi house.\nAccounts differ on what started the battle, Mōri's attached riders shooting at Honda's scouts or the Honda shooting first, and records put the time at 10 a.m., noon or 1 p.m. (here the account starting with Mōri is used). The Sanada spread a false rumor that Asano Nagaakira had turned traitor, shaking the shogunate side, and charged the headquarters two or three times. The banner commander let the standard fall, and some bannermen are said to have fled twelve kilometers. Ogasawara Hidemasa left the field badly wounded and later died; his son Tadanaga was killed. Mizuno Katsunari personally led the first spear to restore the broken Tennōji line, and Akashi Takenori charged the Mizuno troops and vanished.\nShitennō-ji, along with the five-story pagoda Hideyori had rebuilt, had burned in the Winter Campaign the year before, and on this day only the foundation stones remained (it was rebuilt in the Genna era). \"Sanada, the finest warrior in Japan\" is said to be words a Shimazu man wrote home.\nBehind Chausuyama waited the Yamato-road troops who fought the day before and Asano Nagaakira's men. The deployment at Tennōji follows the Japanese Wikipedia article \"Battle of Tennōji-Okayama\" and site guides, and the terrain uses Geospatial Information Authority elevation data (the Uemachi plateau)."],
  ["k16_secret", "This battle is not history. It is the last battle of a what-if war, \"Genna: Turning the Realm\". In the fifth month of Genna 1 (1615), the Toyotomi fell in the Osaka Summer Campaign and Tokugawa Ieyasu became master of the realm. Ieyasu died the next year, and his son Hidetada had held the title of shōgun since Keichō 10 (1605). Tokugawa rule lasted about 260 more years.\nYodo is where the Katsura, Uji and Kizu rivers meet, and the Kyō Kaidō linking Fushimi and Osaka ran along the levee Hideyoshi built on the left bank of the Yodo River. To the east spread Ogura Pond.\nThe story in which Akashi Takenori, who survived the fall of Osaka, the Mōri, Shimazu and Uesugi, whose lands were cut after Sekigahara, and Chōsokabe Morichika answer your call, reach Ieyasu's headquarters on the plain of Yodo, and Ieyasu lays down his sword so that you become ruler of the realm, is a tale made to close your rise from ashigaru. The Tokugawa deployment (vanguard, flank units, the east and west side lines, guns, bannermen, headquarters) and the charge into the headquarters are modeled on Sanada Nobushige splitting the vanguard and closing on Ieyasu's headquarters at Tennōji in the Summer Campaign."],
  ["k17_shuumaku", "This scene is not history. It is a story after the what-if war \"Genna: Turning the Realm\". Historically, after the Osaka Summer Campaign in Genna 1 (1615), the era name was changed to Genna, and the great battles of the Warring States period came to an end (the Genna Peace)."],
  ["d01_kawanakajima", "In the ninth month of Eiroku 4 (1561), Takeda Shingen sent a detached force around behind Uesugi Kenshin on Mount Saijo, and set up his own camp at Hachimanbara. Kenshin saw through this, came down the mountain in the night, and when the fog lifted in the morning he fell on Shingen's main army. In the fierce fighting, Shingen's brother Nobushige, Yamamoto Kansuke and others were killed, but before noon the detached force returned and the Uesugi withdrew across the Sai River."],
  ["d02_minowa", "In Eiroku 9 (1566), Takeda Shingen surrounded Minowa Castle in western Kōzuke with 20,000 men. The lord, Nagano Narimori, sallied out at Wakatahara but was beaten; the attackers broke in through the main gate, and he took his own life in the castle's chapel. With this, Shingen won western Kōzuke."],
  ["d03_mimasetoge", "In Eiroku 12 (1569), after besieging Odawara Castle, Takeda Shingen was ambushed at Mimase Pass on his way back to Kai by the armies of Hōjō Ujiteru and Ujikuni. Shingen kept his pack train guarded while sending Yamagata Masakage's detached force around behind the Hōjō, catching them between and breaking them. On the Takeda side, Asari Nobutane was killed."],
  ["d04_kanbara", "In Eiroku 12 (1569), Takeda Shingen attacked Kanbara Castle in Suruga. Katsuyori and others set fire below the castle to lure out the garrison, beat them when they sallied, pressed straight in, and took the castle in a day. The castle lord Hōjō Ujinobu was killed."],
  ["d05_hanazawa", "In the first month of Genki 1 (1570), Takeda Shingen attacked Hanazawa Castle, held by the Imagawa side in Suruga. The castle stood atop a steep slope, and the garrison fought hard, killing and wounding many attackers, but at last it was made to open. With this, almost no Imagawa castles were left in Suruga."],
  ["d06_futamata", "In Genki 3 (1572), during his westward campaign, Takeda Shingen had Katsuyori and others attack Futamata Castle, held by Nakane Masateru for the Tokugawa. The castle would not fall from the front, so the Takeda floated rafts down the upper Tenryū River to smash the tower over the castle's water source, cut off its water and forced it to open. After this, Shingen fought Ieyasu at Mikatagahara."],
  ["d08_noda", "In the first month of Genki 4 (1573), Takeda Shingen surrounded Noda Castle in Mikawa, held by Suganuma Sadamitsu. The castle was small but tough, and Shingen had miners from Kai cut the veins feeding its wells. Without water the castle opened in the second month, but Shingen's illness grew worse in camp, and the Takeda withdrew. Shingen died in the fourth month on the way home to Kai."],
  ["d09_takatenjin1", "In Tenshō 2 (1574), Takeda Katsuyori surrounded Takatenjin Castle in Tōtōmi with over 20,000 men, a tough mountain castle that even his father Shingen had failed to take. The Takeda built siege towers and took the baileys one by one, and Ieyasu's relief came too late. The castle commander Ogasawara Nobuoki handed over the castle on the 17th day of the sixth month, and Katsuyori made his name."],
  ["d10_nagashinojo", "In the fifth month of Tenshō 3 (1575), Takeda Katsuyori surrounded Nagashino Castle, held by Okudaira Nobumasa, with 15,000 men. The Takeda pressed the Hyōtan and Yagyū baileys and used miners to bring down walls, but the castle held. Torii Suneemon of the garrison slipped through the siege to ask for help at Okazaki, was caught on his way back, and was killed after shouting to the castle that help was coming. A few days later, the great Oda-Tokugawa army arrived at Shitaragahara."],
  ["d11_shitaragahara", "On the 21st day of the fifth month of Tenshō 3 (1575), Takeda Katsuyori attacked the great Oda-Tokugawa army behind its anti-cavalry fences at Shitaragahara. Yamagata Masakage, Naitō Masatoyo, Baba Nobuharu and others charged the fences and were shot down, and the Takeda broke in the early afternoon. Yamagata died before the fence, and Baba while holding the rear. Many of the Takeda's famous generals were lost on this day."],
  ["d12_takatenjin2", "In Tenshō 9 (1581), Ieyasu surrounded Takatenjin Castle for over a year and cut its supply lines. Katsuyori could not send relief, and on the night of the 22nd day of the third month the castle commander Okabe Motonobu sallied out with the garrison and cut into the Tokugawa lines; nearly all were killed. Katsuyori's name fell for sending no relief, and more men left the Takeda."],
  ["d13_toriitoge", "In the second month of Tenshō 10 (1582), when Kiso Yoshimasa went over to the Oda, Katsuyori sent Imafuku Masakazu and others against Kiso. With Oda help, the Kiso forces beat the Takeda at snowy Torii Pass. This was the signal for Oda Nobunaga's invasion of Kai."],
  ["d14_takato", "In Tenshō 10 (1582), during the invasion of Kai, Katsuyori's brother Nishina Morinobu held Takatō Castle with 3,000 men and rejected Oda Nobutada's call to surrender. On the 2nd day of the third month, Nobutada climbed the wall himself in the attack; the castle fell in a day, and Morinobu was killed. It is remembered as the castle that fought to the last for the Takeda."],
  ["d15_tenmokuzan", "In the third month of Tenshō 10 (1582), Takeda Katsuyori, who had burned Shinpu Castle and fled, was betrayed by Oyamada Nobushige too, and headed for Mount Tenmoku, reaching the village of Tano. Caught by Takigawa Kazumasu's troops, Tsuchiya Masatsune cut down many enemies on a narrow path. Katsuyori and his son Nobukatsu took their own lives, and the Takeda were destroyed."],
  ["u01_fuse", "In Tenbun 22 (1553), Murakami Yoshikiyo, driven out by Takeda Harunobu, turned to Nagao Kagetora of Echigo. In the eighth month, Kagetora marched into northern Shinano, beat the Takeda vanguard at Fuse, and won again around Yawata. Harunobu shut himself in Shioda Castle to avoid battle, and Kagetora returned to Echigo in the ninth month. This was the start of the Kawanakajima wars, which lasted twelve years."],
  ["u02_saigawa", "In Tenbun 24 (1555), the Takeda put troops into Asahiyama Castle, and Nagao Kagetora camped east of Zenkō-ji. After skirmishing across the Sai River in the seventh month, the two armies faced each other for 200 days. In the leap tenth month, Imagawa Yoshimoto brokered peace; Asahiyama Castle was torn down and both sides withdrew."],
  ["u03_uenohara", "In the second month of Kōji 3 (1557), the Takeda took Katsurayama Castle in the snow. Kagetora marched to Zenkō-ji in the fourth month and attacked Takeda castles, but Harunobu avoided a decisive battle. In the eighth month the two armies fought at Uenohara without a decision, and Kagetora returned to Echigo in the ninth month."],
  ["u04_odawara", "In the third month of Eiroku 4 (1561), Nagao Kagetora and the lords of the Kantō surrounded Odawara Castle and pushed in as far as the Hasuike gate. Ujiyasu stayed inside and would not come out, and Kagetora lifted the siege after about a month. On the way back, at Tsurugaoka Hachimangū, he took the Uesugi name and the post of Kantō Kanrei, renaming himself Uesugi Masatora."],
  ["u05_hachimanbara", "In the ninth month of Eiroku 4 (1561), Uesugi Masatora (later Kenshin) camped on Mount Saijo. Takeda Shingen planned to strike Saijo with a detached force, but Masatora saw through it, came down the mountain in the night, and on the morning of the 10th, as the fog lifted at Hachimanbara, fell on Shingen's headquarters. In the fierce fighting, Takeda Nobushige, Yamamoto Kansuke and others were killed, but before noon the Takeda detached force returned, and the Uesugi withdrew to Zenkō-ji."],
  ["u06_ikunoyama", "In the autumn of Eiroku 4 (1561), after the fourth battle of Kawanakajima, Uesugi Masatora went out to the Kantō again. Hōjō Ujiyasu, allied with the Takeda, sent troops into northern Musashi, and the two armies fought at Ikunoyama. The Uesugi pushed the Hōjō back, and the Hōjō withdrew south."],
  ["u07_shiozaki", "In Eiroku 7 (1564), Uesugi Terutora marched to Kawanakajima, and Takeda Shingen camped at Shiozaki Castle. The armies faced each other for about sixty days, but no great battle came, and both withdrew. This was the last standoff at Kawanakajima."],
  ["u08_usui", "In Eiroku 9 (1566), Uesugi Terutora led the Kantō lords to surround Usui Castle in Shimōsa. Shirai Nyūdō, sent by the Hōjō to help Hara Tanesada who held the castle, took charge, and the garrison sallied out and broke the attackers. The Uesugi suffered heavy losses, lifted the siege and withdrew. After this, many Kantō lords went over to the Hōjō."],
  ["u09_honjo", "In Eiroku 11 (1568), Honjō Shigenaga sided with Takeda Shingen and rebelled against Uesugi Terutora. Terutora surrounded Honjō Castle in the snow, and Shigenaga harried the siege with night raids. In the third month of the next year, through the mediation of Ashina Moriuji and Date Terumune, Shigenaga surrendered and gave his son Chiyomaru as a hostage."],
  ["u10_shiritarezaka", "In Genki 3 (1572), urged by Takeda Shingen, the Ikkō uprisings of Kaga and Etchū rose and entered Toyama Castle. Uesugi Kenshin marched into Etchū, camped at Shinjō Castle, and soundly beat the advancing uprising forces at Shiritarezaka, south of Toyama Castle. The uprising forces fell back to Toyama Castle."],
  ["u11_nanao", "From Tenshō 4 (1576), Uesugi Kenshin besieged Nanao Castle in Noto. Chō Tsuratatsu, who ran the castle, held firmly and asked Oda Nobunaga for help, but disease spread inside. On the 15th day of the ninth month of Tenshō 5, Yusa Tsugumitsu and others opened the gates from within, and Nanao Castle fell."],
  ["u12_tedorigawa", "In the ninth month of Tenshō 5 (1577), the Oda army under Shibata Katsuie, coming to relieve Nanao Castle, learned it had fallen and withdrew. Uesugi Kenshin chased them from Mattō Castle and struck the retreating Oda at night at the Tedori River, swollen by rain. Many soldiers were swept away by the river."],
  ["u13_otate", "In Tenshō 6 (1578), when Uesugi Kenshin died, his adopted sons Uesugi Kagekatsu and Uesugi Kagetora fought over the succession. Kagekatsu held the main bailey of Kasugayama Castle, and Kagetora entered the Otate residence in Fuchū. Kagekatsu made peace with Takeda Katsuyori and took the Otate in the third month of the next year. Kagetora took his own life at Samegao Castle."],
  ["u14_shibata", "In Tenshō 9 (1581), Shibata Shigeie sided with Oda Nobunaga and rebelled against Uesugi Kagekatsu, fighting for seven years. In Tenshō 15, Kagekatsu took Isamino Castle and attacked Shibata Castle in the tenth month. Shigeie sallied out of the castle and was killed, and the revolt ended."],
  ["u15_hasedo", "In Keichō 5 (1600), Naoe Kanetsugu invaded Mogami Yoshiaki's Dewa and surrounded Hasedō Castle. The castle commander Shimura Akiyasu held well. On the 29th day of the ninth month, word came of the Western defeat at Sekigahara, and Kanetsugu withdrew on the 1st day of the tenth month. He personally led the rear guard against the Mogami and Date pursuit and got back to Yonezawa."],
];
for (const [id, en] of BY_ID) HISTORY_EN.set(id, en);

